  "body": "Research candidates and structured event imports now share the existing 20-item pilot allowance. Database triggers serialize both writers, count linked canonical events once, preserve discard/suppression semantics, and continue known-record refreshes. A global auto-publish setting or research pilot approval alone cannot publish new automated events: PostgreSQL requires separate, durable authority for the exact source and minimum event completeness. Source changes or disablement revoke that authority.\n\nEcuador discovery reads the canonical city registry independently of subscriptions, prioritizes Quito, and retains previously followed destinations. Source enablement is checked again under a lock before writes. Older provider responses cannot overwrite a newer verification, and asynchronous cancellation releases the scheduler lock.\n\nBased on #460 to reuse its unknown-end-time correction; merge that PR first. This migration creates no approval and enables no source. The guarded release manifest, schema verification, and PostgreSQL regression are included. Rollback retains evidence and stops automated sources.\n\nValidation: 28 focused Hspec examples (including schedule/pagination properties), real PostgreSQL mixed-writer capacity race, canonical link deduplication, discard, distinct publication authority, immutable evidence, source-change revocation and rollback/reapplication passed. The full Stack build and 3,538 Hspec examples passed (6 pending), including a final incremental rebuild of the committed source. Required independent review and hosted checks remain mandatory.\n\nThis is a boundary correction, not completion of all ingestion acceptance criteria. Live nationwide source coverage/permissions, event manual/dry-run progress controls, resumable pagination beyond the existing bounded source fetch, and guarded production verification remain follow-up work. The seven existing web-research sources stay manual; Buen Plan access permission is not inferred from public accessibility. Staging/production is still blocked by the previously observed Fly overdue-invoice HTTP 403.\n\n\nReview repair at `697bd58b5ea1c8134be490cce588b65e9a08dad8`: the status endpoint and import preflight share the database canonical-identity capacity count. The existing response field remains compatible. The real PostgreSQL mixed/deduplicated/suppressed count regression, concurrent writer/authority/revocation/rollback tests, 28 focused Hspec examples, actual handler compilation, 82 release tests and all 115 migration-ancestor checks pass. Both original review concerns were addressed and resolved with evidence. Current-head hosted validation and any required renewed approval remain pending. Native stack466 must use an integration approach that preserves shared history and production introduction ancestry; no automatic rebase or deployment was performed.\n\n\nSource-completion follow-up `a125a347cacb8d941a515171e2a81d4c92f2c2d2`: propagates per-event failures and locks/rechecks source enablement before atomic absence reconciliation and success updates, including empty feeds. Rescues PR464 failure propagation with attribution. Real PostgreSQL disabled-source/rollback/enabled-completion regressions and the original boundary suite pass; all 28 discovery tests, actual Cron dependency compilation, catalog gate and specification inventory pass. The third review concern was addressed and resolved with evidence. Current-head CI and renewed review still govern merge readiness; native-stack history constraints remain.\n",
